          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. Write the Lens Maker's formula and explain what each symbol represents.\n2. A plano-convex lens of glass (n = 1.5) has radius of curvature of its curved surface equal to 15 cm. Calculate its focal length and power in air.\n3. What happens to a convex glass lens (n = 1.5) when immersed in a liquid of refractive index (a) 1.5, and (b) 1.65?\n4. An object is placed 30 cm in front of a convex lens of focal length 20 cm. Find the position, magnification, and nature of the image.\n5. A concave lens of focal length 15 cm forms an image 10 cm from the lens. How far is the object placed from the lens?\n6. Two thin lenses of focal lengths +25 cm and −50 cm are kept in contact. Find the equivalent focal length and power of the combination.\n7. A point object is placed in air (n₁ = 1) at a distance of 40 cm in front of a convex spherical glass surface (n₂ = 1.5) of radius of curvature R = +20 cm. Find the position of the image.\n8. If an equiconvex lens of focal length f is cut into two equal halves **perpendicular to the principal axis** (forming two plano-convex lenses), what is the focal length of each half?\n9. State whether True or False: The power of a diverging (concave) lens is always negative.\n10. A convex lens of power +4 D is placed in contact with another lens such that the combination behaves like a diverging lens of focal length 50 cm. Find the power of the second lens.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **1/f = (n₂/n₁ − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂)**, jahan f = focal length, n₂ = lens material ka refractive index, n₁ = surrounding medium ka refractive index, aur R₁, R₂ = dono surfaces ke radii of curvature hain.\n\n2. Plano-convex: R₁ = +15 cm, R₂ = ∞ (1/R₂ = 0):\n   1/f = (1.5 − 1)(1/15 − 0) = 0.5 / 15 = 1 / 30 ⟹ **f = +30 cm**.\n   Power **P = 100 / 30 ≈ +3.33 D**.\n   Jaanch: 0.5 / 15 = 1/30 ⟹ f = 30 cm ✅.\n\n3. (a) **n_l = 1.5 = n_g:** f = ∞ — lens **invisible** ho jaata hai (bina mode light seedhi nikal jaati hai!).\n   (b) **n_l = 1.65 > n_g:** f negative ho jaati hai — convex lens **diverging (concave) lens** ban jaata hai!\n\n4. u = −30 cm, f = +20 cm:\n   1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/20 + 1/(−30) = (3 − 2)/60 = +1/60 ⟹ **v = +60 cm** (Real & inverted).\n   m = v/u = (+60)/(−30) = **−2** (2× magnified, inverted).\n   Jaanch: 1/60 − 1/(−30) = 3/60 = 1/20 ✅.\n\n5. Concave lens hamesha virtual image usi side banata hai ⟹ f = −15 cm, v = −10 cm:\n   1/u = 1/v − 1/f = 1/(−10) − 1/(−15) = −1/10 + 1/15 = (−3 + 2)/30 = −1/30 ⟹ **u = −30 cm** (30 cm in front of lens).\n   Jaanch: 1/(−10) − 1/(−30) = −2/30 = −1/15 ✅.\n\n6. 1/f_eq = 1/25 + 1/(−50) = (2 − 1)/50 = +1/50 ⟹ **f_eq = +50 cm**.\n   **P_eq = 100 / 50 = +2.0 D** (converging).\n   Jaanch: P₁ = +4 D, P₂ = −2 D ⟹ +4 − 2 = +2 D ✅.\n\n7. n₂/v − n₁/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R ⟹ 1.5/v − 1/(−40) = (1.5 − 1)/20 = 0.5/20 = 1/40.\n   1.5/v + 1/40 = 1/40 ⟹ 1.5/v = 0 ⟹ **v = ∞** (rays glass ke andar principal axis ke parallel ho jaati hain!).\n   Jaanch: 1/40 − 1/40 = 0 ⟹ v = ∞ ✅.\n\n8. Original equiconvex: 1/f = (n − 1)(2/R). Har plano-convex half: 1/f' = (n − 1)(1/R − 0) = ½ (1/f) ⟹ **f' = 2 f** (focal length double ho jaati hai!).\n\n9. **True ✅.** Concave lens ki focal length f negative hoti hai ⟹ P = 1/f hamesha **negative**!\n\n10. Combined diverging focal length = −50 cm ⟹ P_eq = 100 / (−50) = −2 D.\n    P_eq = P₁ + P₂ ⟹ −2 = +4 + P₂ ⟹ **P₂ = −6.0 D**.\n    Jaanch: +4 D + (−6 D) = −2 D ✅.\n\n---\n\nAb tum Spherical Surface Refraction, Lens Maker's Formula aur Lens Combinations ke saare numericals master kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Glass Prism se white light ka 7 rangon mein bikharna — **Prism Formula**, **Minimum Deviation**, aur **Dispersion** seekhenge!",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. Write the Lens Maker's formula and explain what each symbol represents.\n2. A plano-convex lens of glass (n = 1.5) has radius of curvature of its curved surface equal to 15 cm. Calculate its focal length and power in air.\n3. What happens to a convex glass lens (n = 1.5) when immersed in a liquid of refractive index (a) 1.5, and (b) 1.65?\n4. An object is placed 30 cm in front of a convex lens of focal length 20 cm. Find the position, magnification, and nature of the image.\n5. A concave lens of focal length 15 cm forms an image 10 cm from the lens. How far is the object placed from the lens?\n6. Two thin lenses of focal lengths +25 cm and −50 cm are kept in contact. Find the equivalent focal length and power of the combination.\n7. A point object is placed in air (n₁ = 1) at a distance of 40 cm in front of a convex spherical glass surface (n₂ = 1.5) of radius of curvature R = +20 cm. Find the position of the image.\n8. If an equiconvex lens of focal length f is cut into two equal halves **perpendicular to the principal axis** (forming two plano-convex lenses), what is the focal length of each half?\n9. State whether True or False: The power of a diverging (concave) lens is always negative.\n10. A convex lens of power +4 D is placed in contact with another lens such that the combination behaves like a diverging lens of focal length 50 cm. Find the power of the second lens.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. **1/f = (n₂/n₁ − 1)(1/R₁ − 1/R₂)**, jahan f = focal length, n₂ = lens material ka refractive index, n₁ = surrounding medium ka refractive index, aur R₁, R₂ = dono surfaces ke radii of curvature hain.\n\n2. Plano-convex: R₁ = +15 cm, R₂ = ∞ (1/R₂ = 0):\n   1/f = (1.5 − 1)(1/15 − 0) = 0.5 / 15 = 1 / 30 ⟹ **f = +30 cm**.\n   Power **P = 100 / 30 ≈ +3.33 D**.\n   Jaanch: 0.5 / 15 = 1/30 ⟹ f = 30 cm ✅.\n\n3. (a) **n_l = 1.5 = n_g:** f = ∞ — lens **invisible** ho jaata hai (bina mode light seedhi nikal jaati hai!).\n   (b) **n_l = 1.65 > n_g:** f negative ho jaati hai — convex lens **diverging (concave) lens** ban jaata hai!\n\n4. u = −30 cm, f = +20 cm:\n   1/v = 1/f + 1/u = 1/20 + 1/(−30) = (3 − 2)/60 = +1/60 ⟹ **v = +60 cm** (Real & inverted).\n   m = v/u = (+60)/(−30) = **−2** (2× magnified, inverted).\n   Jaanch: 1/60 − 1/(−30) = 3/60 = 1/20 ✅.\n\n5. Concave lens hamesha virtual image usi side banata hai ⟹ f = −15 cm, v = −10 cm:\n   1/u = 1/v − 1/f = 1/(−10) − 1/(−15) = −1/10 + 1/15 = (−3 + 2)/30 = −1/30 ⟹ **u = −30 cm** (30 cm in front of lens).\n   Jaanch: 1/(−10) − 1/(−30) = −2/30 = −1/15 ✅.\n\n6. 1/f_eq = 1/25 + 1/(−50) = (2 − 1)/50 = +1/50 ⟹ **f_eq = +50 cm**.\n   **P_eq = 100 / 50 = +2.0 D** (converging).\n   Jaanch: P₁ = +4 D, P₂ = −2 D ⟹ +4 − 2 = +2 D ✅.\n\n7. n₂/v − n₁/u = (n₂ − n₁)/R ⟹ 1.5/v − 1/(−40) = (1.5 − 1)/20 = 0.5/20 = 1/40.\n   1.5/v + 1/40 = 1/40 ⟹ 1.5/v = 0 ⟹ **v = ∞** (rays glass ke andar principal axis ke parallel ho jaati hain!).\n   Jaanch: 1/40 − 1/40 = 0 ⟹ v = ∞ ✅.\n\n8. Original equiconvex: 1/f = (n − 1)(2/R). Har plano-convex half: 1/f' = (n − 1)(1/R − 0) = ½ (1/f) ⟹ **f' = 2 f** (focal length double ho jaati hai!).\n\n9. **True ✅.** Concave lens ki focal length f negative hoti hai ⟹ P = 1/f hamesha **negative**!\n\n10. Combined diverging focal length = −50 cm ⟹ P_eq = 100 / (−50) = −2 D.\n    P_eq = P₁ + P₂ ⟹ −2 = +4 + P₂ ⟹ **P₂ = −6.0 D**.\n    Jaanch: +4 D + (−6 D) = −2 D ✅.\n\n---\n\nAb tum Spherical Surface Refraction, Lens Maker's Formula aur Lens Combinations ke saare numericals master kar chuke ho!\n\n**Agle topic mein:** Glass Prism se white light ka 7 rangon mein bikharna — **Prism Formula**, **Minimum Deviation**, aur **Dispersion** seekhenge!"
          }
